// Pashto (ps) — Eastern Iranian, Perso-Arabic ABJAD (extended). wikipron pus (human) PRIMARY + kaikki pus (human)
// SECONDARY. Like Urdu/Persian, SHORT vowels are usually unwritten → folded (unrecoverable): a~ə~i~u default-vowel
// quality. Also folds: dental t̪~t / retroflex notation, r~ɻ, the NARROW→BROAD ښ/ږ notation (ʂ~ʃ, ʐ~ʒ), and length.
//
// ⚠ EVERY PASHTO REFEREE WE HAVE IS A MACROLANGUAGE AGGREGATE, AND THIS ENGINE IS ONE VARIETY. ISO 639-3 `pus`
// is a macrolanguage — pbt (Southern/Kandahari), pbu (Northern/Peshawar), pst (Central/Waziri) — and wikipron,
// kaikki and espeak-ng's ps_list are all aggregated over it. `pashto.ts` declares a single variety ("ښ/ږ =
// Kandahari retroflex ʂ/ʐ"), so the umbrella marks a correct Southern reading wrong wherever the aggregate
// happens to carry only a Northern one. ⚠ AND THE FOLDS CANNOT PAPER OVER IT: merging the Northern x (ښ) or
// ɡ (ږ) into our ʂ/ʐ would also merge خ and ګ, which are real phoneme contrasts. Only the NARROW→BROAD
// notation folds are legitimate (ʂ→ʃ, ʐ→ʒ), and those are what remain below.
//
// So the primary DELIBERATELY STAYS THE AGGREGATE — it is the honest floor and must not be gamed — and the
// variety slices sit beside it as secondaries, cut by tools/pashto/split_referee_by_dialect.py on the ښ/ږ
// isogloss alone (ښ→ʂ/x/ç, ږ→ʐ/ɡ/ʝ; a broad ʃ/ʒ is UNCOMMITTED and stays in every slice).
//
// ⚠ THE VALIDATION IS THE HIT COUNT, NOT THE PERCENTAGE. A variety filter must not remove anything the engine
// was already scoring; if it does, it is a difficulty filter wearing a linguistic label. Ours: 744 → 744 on
// wikipron and 666 → 666 on kaikki, so the whole gain is denominator. And the engine scores HIGHEST on its own
// variety — pbt 63.0% / pst 60.4% / pbu 58.1% — which is the second check that the slices are real.
// (Those three are the Run-8 measurement, kept as the pair-wise ORDERING evidence they were cut to be; the
// pbt figure has since moved to 46.9% non-circular / 69.6% shipped — Runs 10 and 16 — while pst/pbu were
// not re-run on either basis.)
//
// ⚠ THE AGGREGATE LEANS NORTHERN, WHICH IS THE REAL REASON THE UMBRELLA SCORE IS LOW. Counting the isogloss
// across the primary: ښ is x(pbu) 138 · ʃ(broad) 99 · ʂ(pbt) 52 · ç(pst) 32, and ږ is ɡ(pbu) 151 · ʒ 84 ·
// ʐ(pbt) 28 · ʝ(pst) 12. kaikki is the same shape (x 90 / ʂ 21, ɡ 119 / ʐ 17). So a SOUTHERN engine is being
// graded against a reference that answers Northern roughly 3:1 on the diagnostic letters — the umbrella is
// not a neutral average, it is mostly another variety.
//
// ⚠ AND kaikki CARRIES ITS OWN DIALECT TAGS, so for that source the assignment is READ rather than inferred —
// tools/pashto/build_kaikki_dialect_referees.py. Those tagged referees are what finally show the split is
// real rather than plausible. A SOUTHERN engine on ground-truth-labelled data:
//
//     pbt (ours)  50/97  = 51.5%        pbu  2/102 = 2.0%        pst (not re-run)
//
// A 26x separation between our variety and Northern. That is the number to quote when anyone asks why the
// `pus` aggregate reads 42.5%: the umbrella is ~3:1 Northern on the isogloss, and this engine scores 2.0%
// there. ⚠ AND THE SEPARATION WIDENED RATHER THAN NARROWED WHEN THE ENGINE IMPROVED — Run 10's carrier rules
// took pbt 67.0% → 77.3% on the shipped basis and left pbu unmoved. An engine change that raised BOTH would
// have been evidence the split was a difficulty artifact; this is the control that says it is a real one.
// ⚠ The tags also VALIDATE the wikipron inference — ښ is pbt→ʂ 88% / pbu→x 94%, ږ is pbt→ʐ 94% /
// pbu→ɡ 91% — which is what licenses split_referee_by_dialect.py on a source that has no tags.
//
// ⚠⚠ THE NUMBERS BELOW ARE NON-CIRCULAR AS OF 2026-08-11, AND THEY DROPPED ~20 POINTS WHEN THAT WAS FIXED.
// The engine's coverage layer (`pashto/lexicon.tsv`) is mined by tools/perso-arabic/invert_harakat.ts from
// `silver.tsv` and `silver.kaikki.tsv` — which ARE wikipron and kaikki, i.e. the referees below. Scoring the
// shipped `phonemizeWord` therefore graded the referee's own answers. eval.ts now excludes every lexicon row
// whose KEY came from those pools (`psNonCircular`), which is precise rather than merely cautious: the miner
// reads the CC-BY-SA silver FIRST and keeps one vocalization per skeleton, so for any word wikipron/kaikki
// also covers, the referee-derived reading is the one that shipped. Measured on ps.wikipron-pbt.tsv:
//
//     shipped lexicon (a COVERAGE statistic, not engine quality)   892/1281 = 69.6%
//     NON-CIRCULAR — what this file now reports                    601/1281 = 46.9%
//     rules only, no lexicon at all                                601/1281 = 46.9%
//
// ⚠ The last two being equal is a real result, not a bug: the espeak and ps.wiktionary tranches together move
// this referee by ZERO words, because they barely reach its vocabulary (espeak supplies a row for 51 of 1,281).
// Their value is production coverage, which no referee here can see. 432 of 14,021 lexicon rows (3.1%) are
// excluded; referee words holding a referee-derived entry go 503 → 0.
//
// The 22.7pp gap is the referee's own answers fed back. The espeak tranche is 97.4% of the lexicon and moves
// this eval by −3 words — because it supplies an entry for only 51/1281 (4%) of the referee's vocabulary. (154
// referee words are in espeak's pool at all, but for ~103 of them our g2p already agrees, so the export emits
// no row.) That is coverage, not quality: espeak's value is production tokens, which this eval cannot see. ⚠ Compare ENGINE changes on the
// number this file now reports (the three carrier rules of Run 10: 42.2% → 46.9%), never on the shipped one.
// ⚠ The four languages with the same hazard (en-GB, km, af, ilo, and gu) each export a `phonemizeWordRules`.
// ps does NOT, on purpose: 95.4% of its lexicon is espeak and 548 rows are ps.wiktionary, sources no referee
// has seen, and a rule-only eval would discard them. Key-exclusion keeps them and drops only the feedback.
//
// ⚠ TAGS ARE SPARSE (305 of 1,644 kaikki IPA records). The untagged remainder goes to ps.kaikki-untagged.tsv
// and is NOT distributed into the three — an unlabelled reading is evidence about Pashto, not about a member.
// pst stays thin everywhere (43 tagged words; ç ×32 / ʝ ×12 in wikipron), so do not read a pst score as
// strong evidence about Waziri.
{
    "referees": [
        { "file": "ps.wikipron-pus-broad.tsv", "source": "wikipron pus_arab broad (human) — MACROLANGUAGE aggregate, the honest floor", "role": "primary" },
        { "file": "ps.kaikki-pus.tsv", "source": "kaikki pus (Wiktionary, human)", "role": "secondary" },
        { "file": "ps.wikipron-pbt.tsv", "source": "wikipron pus → pbt (Southern) slice — our variety, 601/1281 = 46.9% NON-CIRCULAR (69.6% with the referee-derived lexicon rows, which is coverage not quality)", "role": "secondary" },
        { "file": "ps.kaikki-pbt-tagged.tsv", "source": "kaikki pbt (Southern) — assigned by kaikki's OWN dialect tags, GROUND TRUTH: 50/97 = 51.5% NON-CIRCULAR (77.3% shipped)", "role": "secondary" },
        { "file": "ps.kaikki-pbu-tagged.tsv", "source": "kaikki pbu (Northern) — ground truth, and the proof the split is real: 2/102 = 2.0% NON-CIRCULAR — a 26x separation from our own variety's 51.5%", "role": "secondary" }
    ],
    "segmentJoin": true,
    "folds": [
        {
            "pattern": "(.)\\1",
            "replace": "$1",
            "note": "geminate as length ː (ours) vs doubled symbol (referee) — notation (as pa)"
        },
        { "pattern": "t̪", "replace": "t", "note": "dental ⟨ت/ط/د⟩ notation (referee-variable)" },
        { "pattern": "d̪", "replace": "d", "note": "dental notation" },
        { "pattern": "[əaiu]", "replace": "ə", "note": "SHORT vowel quality a~ə~i~u — UNRECOVERABLE from the undiacritized abjad (the deferred restoration subsystem)" },
        { "pattern": "ɪ", "replace": "ə", "note": "referee short ɪ = our default short vowel" },
        { "pattern": "ʊ", "replace": "ə", "note": "referee short ʊ = default short vowel" },
        { "pattern": "ʂ", "replace": "ʃ", "note": "ښ: our narrow Kandahari ʂ vs the referee's BROAD ʃ — a transcription-granularity fold, not a dialect one. ⚠ `ç` was REMOVED from this class on 2026-08-10: it is the CENTRAL/Waziri reflex, so folding it in merged pbt with pst — a dialect merge hiding inside a notation rule. MEASURED IMPACT: ZERO, on every referee. Central-only entries are rare (ç ×32 in wikipron, ×2 in kaikki) and the ones that exist also list a ʂ or broad-ʃ variant, which still credits us. Kept because it is right in principle and costs nothing, and stated as zero-impact so nobody re-derives it as a win." },
        { "pattern": "ʐ", "replace": "ʒ", "note": "ږ: our narrow ʐ vs the referee's broad ʒ — notation only. Unlike the ښ rule above this one never merged dialects: the Northern ɡ and Central ʝ are untouched by it, so it stays as written." },
        { "pattern": "[ɻɽ]", "replace": "r", "note": "retroflex flap ⟨ړ⟩ ɻ ~ r notation" }
    ]
}
